.TH SG 1 "2026-08-30" "small_git 0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
sg \- small_git: a git-object-format-compatible version control tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B sg
.I command
.RI [ args ...]
.br
.B sg
.RB ( -h | --help | help )
.br
.B sg
.RB ( -v | --version | version )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sg
is a from-scratch, pure C implementation of a version control tool that
reads and writes the same on-disk object format, packfile format, and index
format as
.BR git (1).
A repository initialized by
.B sg
can be operated on by the real
.B git
binary and vice versa, in the same
.B .git
directory, as long as
.B sg
specific opt-in features (content-defined chunking; see
.B chunk-info
below and the
.B FILES
section) are not in use.
.PP
.B sg
implements a focused subset of git's porcelain and plumbing commands. It
does not attempt to be a drop-in replacement for every git flag or
subcommand; only the commands and options listed below are recognized. In
particular, most long-form git options (e.g.
.BR "git log --oneline" ,
.BR "git commit --amend" )
have no
.B sg
equivalent unless explicitly documented here.
.PP
Every subcommand that touches the working directory or index requires being
run inside a repository (a directory containing, or with an ancestor
containing, a
.B .git
directory), the same discovery rule git itself uses.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS init
.B sg init
.RI [ directory ]
.PP
Creates a new repository. If
.I directory
is given it is created (if necessary) and initialized there; otherwise the
current directory is used. Prints
.RI "\(dqInitialized empty Git repository in " dir "/.git/\(dq" .
.SS hash-object
.B sg hash-object
.RB [ -w ]
.RB [ -t
.IR type ]
.RB [ --stdin ]
.I file
.PP
Computes the object id of a file's content (or, with
.BR --stdin ", of standard input)."
.B -t
.I type
selects the object type used to compute the hash (default
.BR blob ).
.B -w
additionally writes the object into the repository's object store as a
loose object.
.I file
is required unless
.B --stdin
is given. The whole input is read into memory before hashing; there is no
streaming mode.
.SS cat-file
.B sg cat-file
.RB ( -t | -s | -p )
.I object
.PP
Inspects a single object by its full 40-character hex id (abbreviated ids
are not accepted).
.B -t
prints the object's type,
.B -s
prints its size in bytes, and
.B -p
pretty-prints its content: for a
.B tree
object, each entry is printed as
.IR "mode type hash\etname" ;
for
.B blob
and
.B commit
objects, the object's raw stored bytes are written to stdout unmodified.
If the object is a blob whose content is an
.BR sg -internal
chunked-storage pointer (see
.BR chunk-info ),
a note is printed to stderr explaining this; stdout still receives the
pointer's own raw text, never the reassembled file content \(em
.B cat-file
never resolves chunk pointers.
.SS add
.B sg add
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Stages files into the index. Each
.I path
must resolve inside the repository; paths outside the repository are
rejected. A
.I path
naming a directory is walked recursively (entries are processed in sorted
order), so
.B "sg add ."
stages the whole tree from the current directory down. The walk never
descends into a
.B .git
directory at any depth, and files matched by the ignore rules described
under
.B .gitignore
below are skipped silently \(em except that an already-tracked file is
always staged, because ignore rules never apply to tracked content.
.PP
Naming an ignored path explicitly on the command line is an error rather
than a silent no-op; pass
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
to stage it anyway. A
.B --
argument ends option parsing, so a file literally named
.B -f
can be staged.
.PP
A
.I path
that no longer exists but is still recorded in the index stages its
deletion. Under a directory argument, the deletion of any tracked file
beneath it is staged the same way, matching
.BR "git add <dir>" .
.PP
Either every named path stages successfully or the index is left
completely untouched: the index is written only after all arguments have
been processed without error.
.PP
Remaining differences from
.BR "git add" :
.IP \(bu 2
.B Symlinks are skipped, not staged.
A symlink argument prints a warning (\(dqis a symlink, skipping
(unsupported)\(dq) and is not added to the index; this is not an error for
the overall command.
.IP \(bu 2
.B Only regular files are staged.
A path that is not a regular file (FIFO, socket, device) is reported as an
error when named explicitly, and skipped during a directory walk. There is
no
.BR "git add -p" ,
no pathspec globbing, and no
.BR "git add -A" /\%\fB-u\fR
distinction.
.IP \(bu 2
If a path currently carries an unresolved merge conflict (stage 1/2/3
entries in the index), staging it clears the conflict and records the
working-directory content as the resolution \(em this is how
.B sg
expects conflicts to be marked resolved during
.B merge
or
.BR rebase .
.PP
If chunked storage is enabled for the repository (see
.B FILES
below) and a file's content is at or above the configured threshold, its
blob is written using content-defined chunking instead of as a single
ordinary blob.
.SS commit
.B sg commit -m
.I message
.PP
Creates a commit from the current index content, updating the current
branch to point at it.
.B -m
.I message
is required; there is no editor-based message flow, no
.BR --amend ,
and no way to sign or author-override a commit via flags (see
.B ENVIRONMENT
for author/committer identity).
.I message
is normalized the same way real git's default
.B --cleanup=whitespace
does: trailing whitespace is stripped from each line, runs of blank lines
are collapsed to one, and leading/trailing blank lines are dropped. If the
result is empty (including an all-whitespace
.IR message ),
.B sg
refuses to create the commit, matching real git.
.PP
Fails if the index has unresolved merge conflicts (stage 1/2/3 entries) or
if a rebase is currently in progress (use
.B "sg rebase --continue"
instead). If a merge is in progress (\c
.B MERGE_HEAD
present) and all conflicts are resolved, the resulting commit is a merge
commit with two parents. If
.B MERGE_HEAD
is present but its contents cannot be parsed as a commit id, the commit is
refused outright rather than quietly recorded with a single parent \(em a
merge silently vanishing from the history is a worse outcome than a failed
command. Real git refuses the same way (\(dqCorrupt MERGE_HEAD file\(dq).
Use
.B "sg merge --abort"
to discard the broken merge state.
.SS log
.B sg log
.RB [ -n <count> | -<count> | --max-count= <count> ]
.RB [ --oneline ]
.RB [ --pretty [= <fmt> ]| --format= <fmt> ]
.RB [ -p | --patch ]
.RB [ --stat ]
.RI [ rev ]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Prints the commit history starting at
.B HEAD
(or
.IR rev ,
if given), one commit per entry, in the familiar
.B "git log"
default format.
.B "sg log"
walks
.B first-parent only
\(em when a merge commit is encountered its other parents are not
themselves visited (though the commit is flagged with a
.B Merge:
line listing all of its parents, in the default format).
.BI -n " count"
(also
.BI - count
and
.BI --max-count= count )
limits how many entries are shown;
.B "-n 0"
is legal and prints nothing.
.B --oneline
prints
.RI "<abbrev> " subject
instead of the full header block.
.B -p
(or
.BR --patch )
and
.B --stat
attach each entry's own diff against its first parent (the empty tree for a
root commit), the same rendering
.B "sg show"
uses.
.PP
An optional
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ path ...]
restricts which commits are shown to those that changed something under
one of the given paths, compared against
.B each commit's own first parent
\(em the same restriction
.B -p / --stat
applies to a single entry's diff, applied here to the walk itself. A
pathspec also restricts
.B -p / --stat 's
own diff to the same paths. The matching rules are the same three ordered
clauses
.B "sg diff"
uses (exact path, leading directory, or wildcard \(em see
.B "sg diff"
above); pathspec magic
.RB ( :(icase) ,
.BR :! ,
.BR :/ )
is rejected outright, matching nothing or matching a file literally
starting with
.B :
would both silently answer a question that was never asked. A bare
argument (no
.BR -- )
follows the same revision/path disambiguation
.B "sg diff"
uses: an argument that is both a valid revision and an existing path is
rejected as ambiguous, and once the first path-like argument is seen every
later argument must itself exist.
.PP
There is no
.BR --graph ,
.BR --follow ,
.BR --all ,
.BR --reverse ,
.BR --date= ,
.BR --author= / --grep= ,
.BR -c / --cc ,
.BR --full-history ,
.BR --simplify-merges ,
or a second
.IR rev .
.PP
.B --pretty
and
.B --format
select the entry's rendering; see
.B "sg show"
below for the full grammar and the seven built-in format names \(em both
commands share the same renderer and the same rules.
.SS show
.B sg show
.RB [ -s ]
.RB [ -p | --patch ]
.RB [ --stat ]
.RB [ --name-only | --name-status ]
.RB [ --oneline ]
.RB [ --pretty [= <fmt> ]| --format= <fmt> ]
.RI [ object ...]
.PP
Shows one or more objects: a commit (the default entry format, shared with
.BR "sg log" ), an annotated tag (its own header and message, followed by the
object it points at), a tree (its entry listing, directories suffixed with
.BR / ), a blob (its raw content), or
.RI < rev >: <path>
(the blob or tree at that path). With no arguments, shows
.BR HEAD .
The same commit is never printed twice in one invocation, even when named
twice or reached both directly and through a tag; every other object type
is printed once per argument.
.PP
For a commit,
.B -p
(or
.BR --patch ", the default when no format flag is given)" ,
.BR --stat ", " --name-only " and " --name-status
select how its diff against the first parent is rendered (the empty tree,
for a root commit), the same four machine formats
.B "sg diff"
uses.
.B --oneline
prints
.RI "<abbrev> " subject
instead of the full header block.
.B -s
suppresses the diff entirely, printing only the header.
.PP
These five flags are not simply \(dqlast one wins\(dq:
.B -s
clears every one of the other four, while
.BR -p / --stat
each set their own bit and also cancel a previous
.BR -s ,
and
.BR --name-only / --name-status
each set their own bit without cancelling
.BR -s .
So
.B "-s -p"
prints a patch (the
.B -p
cancels
.BR -s ),
.B "-p -s"
prints nothing but the header, and
.B "--name-only -s"
also prints nothing but the header (bit-for-bit: 16 flag combinations were
measured against real git 2.55.0 to pin this exact model, since it is not
guessable from a single flag at a time).
.B --name-only
and
.B --name-status
may not both be given, and neither may combine with
.B -s
unless a later
.B -p / --stat / --name-only / --name-status
overrides it \(em `sg show -s --name-only` is an error, but
`sg show --name-only -s -p` is not (the
.B -s
already cleared
.B --name-only
before
.B -p
set its own bit).
.PP
A merge commit's diff is rendered against parent 1's tree as a combined diff
(git's
.BR "diff --cc" ),
the same restriction
.B "sg log -p"
has: only a two-parent merge can render a
.B -p
patch, and an octopus (more than two parents) whose combined diff would be
non-empty is refused outright.
.B --stat
has no such restriction \(em it is always a first-parent diff, at any parent
count.
.BR --name-only " and " --name-status
also have no such restriction: an octopus's dense row set (the same set
.B -p
would use) is rendered with one status letter
.I per parent
(so a three-parent merge might print
.BR MMM ,
not just
.BR MM ),
never refused. This dense row set differs from
.BR --stat 's
first-parent set \(em a path can appear in one and not the other.
.PP
.B --pretty
and
.B --format
select the entry's rendering, shared with
.BR "sg log" .
The value is one of the seven built-in names
.BR oneline ", " short ", " medium " (the default), " full ", " fuller ", "
.BR raw " and " reference ,
matched case-insensitively; or
.BI format: str
/
.BI tformat: str
for a user format string (case-sensitive prefixes); or a bare string
containing a
.B %
placeholder, which is treated the same as
.BI tformat: str .
A user format string may use the placeholders
.BR %H / %h / %T / %t / %P / %p
(object ids; the abbreviated forms use 7 hex digits, and
.BR %P / %p
list every parent space-separated),
.BR %an / %ae / %al / %ad / %aD / %at / %ai / %aI / %as
and the committer equivalents
.BR %cn / %ce / %cl / %cd / %cD / %ct / %ci / %cI / %cs
(name, email, email's local part before
.BR @ ,
and six renderings of the same timestamp \(em
.B %ad
/
.B %cd
git's default format,
.B %aD
/
.B %cD
RFC 2822,
.B %at
/
.B %ct
the raw Unix timestamp,
.B %ai
/
.B %ci
ISO 8601 with a space,
.B %aI
/
.B %cI
strict ISO 8601 with a
.B T
separator, and
.B %as
/
.B %cs
just the date), and
.BR %s / %f / %b / %B
(subject, a filename-safe sanitized subject, the body after the first
blank line, and the raw message), plus the literal escapes
.BR %n " (newline), " %% " (a literal " % "), and " %xNN
(the raw byte given by hex digits
.IR NN ).
Every timestamp placeholder shows the clock shifted into the timestamp's
own stored offset, not UTC and not the machine's local time. Any
.B %
sequence outside this table is refused with a diagnostic naming it and a
non-zero exit \(em real git instead prints such a sequence literally or as
empty, which is a deliberate, measured divergence (see
.B CAVEATS
below).
.B --pretty
with no
.RI = fmt
means
.BR medium ;
bare
.B --format
with no
.RI = fmt
is a usage error.
.B oneline
prints the full 40-hex id (unlike
.BR --oneline ,
which abbreviates); the two flags are independent of each other.
Between an entry and its own diff,
.B oneline
and
.BI format: str
add no separator at all, while
.BI tformat: str
and every other built-in add the usual blank line (or
.B ---
when both
.B --stat
and
.B -p
are given). Between entries in
.BR "sg log" ,
.BI format: str
joins with a single newline and adds none after the last entry;
.BI tformat: str
and
.B oneline
terminate each entry with nothing extra between them; every other built-in
keeps the usual blank line between entries.
.PP
Real git always writes
.B HEAD
as the \(dqours\(dq label of a merge conflict's markers and message;
.B sg
writes the current branch's name instead (falling back to
.B HEAD
when detached), which is a deliberate, measured divergence \(em see
.B CAVEATS
below.
.SS status
.B sg status
.RB [ -s | --short | --porcelain ]
.RB [ -b | --branch ]
.RB [ --ignored ]
.RB [ -u <mode> | --untracked-files= <mode> ]
.PP
Prints the current branch, whether a merge or rebase is in progress (with
next-step hints), and three sections of changes: staged (index vs.
.BR HEAD ),
unstaged (working directory vs. index), and untracked files \(em each
section includes a short hint about the relevant follow-up command, such as
.B add
or
.BR restore .
.PP
Unresolved merge conflicts are listed under \(dqUnmerged paths\(dq, labelled
by which of index stages 1/2/3 the path carries:
.BR "both deleted" ,
.BR "deleted by them" ,
.BR "both modified" ,
.BR "deleted by us" ,
.BR "added by us" ,
.BR "both added" ,
or
.BR "added by them" .
.PP
.BR -s ", " --short " and " --porcelain
select git's short format: one
.RI XY " " path
line per entry, where
.I X
is the index against
.BR HEAD ,
.I Y
is the working directory against the index,
.B ??
marks an untracked path and
.B !!
an ignored one. Tracked entries come first, then untracked, then ignored,
each group sorted by path; a path can appear twice (for instance
.B "D  f"
and
.BR "?? f" ,
after the file has been removed from the index but left on disk).
.PP
The short format quotes a path that merely contains a space, because its
.B "?? "
prefix makes a space a field separator; the long format leaves the same name
bare. Both quote control characters. This is a real difference between the
two formats in git, not an inconsistency in
.BR sg .
.PP
.BR -b " and " --branch
add a leading
.RI "## " branch
line, which reads
.B "## No commits yet on <branch>"
on an unborn
.B HEAD
and
.B "## HEAD (no branch)"
when detached.
.B --ignored
adds ignored paths.
.PP
.BR -u <mode> " and " --untracked-files= <mode>
control how untracked paths are listed.
.B normal
(the default) folds a directory that contains no tracked path into a single
.RI dir /
entry;
.B all
lists every file individually; and
.B no
omits untracked paths entirely. A bare
.B -u
means
.BR all .
The mode must be attached to
.B -u
without a space \(em
.B "-u no"
treats
.B no
as a pathspec, as it does in git.
.PP
Folding is decided per directory and recursively: a directory holding a
tracked path anywhere beneath it is descended into rather than folded, a
directory whose contents are all ignored is omitted unless
.B --ignored
is given (in which case it too is folded), and an empty directory produces
nothing. Ignored files sitting directly inside a folded untracked directory
are still listed one by one under
.BR --ignored .
.PP
The untracked list honours the ignore rules described under
.B .gitignore
in
.B FILES
below: ignored files are omitted and ignored directories are not descended
into. Tracked files are never affected by ignore rules. If a directory
cannot be read (for instance a tree nested deeper than the platform's
.BR PATH_MAX ,
which
.B sg
cannot traverse because it composes absolute paths), a warning is printed
to standard error saying the list may be incomplete, rather than the entry
being dropped silently.
.SS diff
.B sg diff
.RB [ --cached | --staged ]
.RB [ --stat [= <width> [, <name-width> ]]| --numstat | --shortstat | --name-only | --name-status ]
.RB [ -M [ <n> ]| -C [ <n> ]| --find-copies-harder | --no-renames ]
.RB [ -c | --cc ]
.RB [ --histogram ]
.RI [ rev " [" rev ]]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Shows a difference between two of: a commit's tree, the index, and the
working directory. With no arguments it compares the index to the working
directory, as
.B "git diff"
does. With
.BR --cached " (or " --staged )
it compares
.B HEAD
\(em or
.IR rev ,
if one is given \(em to the index. With one
.I rev
it compares that commit's tree to the working directory, and with two it
compares the two trees. Flags may appear anywhere on the command line.
.PP
When a
.I rev
is given, the
.I index
decides which paths take part, not the working directory: a path recorded in
the tree but absent from the index is reported as deleted even though the
file is still on disk, and an untracked file is not reported at all. This
matches git.
.PP
A path with an unresolved conflict is reported as git reports it, which
differs by comparison:
.B U
under
.BR --cached ,
an ordinary
.B M
against a
.I rev
(the index only decides membership there; the content still comes from the
working directory), and, for the plain index-vs-working-directory case, a
combined diff (see below) when both sides of the conflict (index stage 2
and stage 3) are present, or
.B "* Unmerged path"
plus an ordinary row comparing stage 2 against the file on disk when only
one of them is.
.PP
The default output is a patch.
.BR --numstat ", " --shortstat ", " --name-only " and " --name-status
produce git's machine-readable formats byte for byte;
.B --stat
produces git's diffstat, including its column layout, bar scaling, name
truncation and display-width handling for wide characters, and honours
.B COLUMNS
the way git does even when standard output is not a terminal.
.BI --stat= <width>
and
.BI --stat= <width> , <name-width>
override the widths.
.PP
Files whose content contains a NUL byte are reported as \(dqBinary
files ... differ\(dq in a patch, and as
.RI "Bin " old " -> " new " bytes"
in
.BR --stat .
A change to nothing but the mode prints
.B "old mode"
and
.B "new mode"
lines with no hunk.
.PP
Trailing
.I path
arguments restrict the output to the paths they cover. They are resolved
against the current directory, not the repository root, and need not exist:
naming a deleted file is the only way to ask about it. Three rules decide a
match, and they are git's:
an exact match; a leading directory, so
.B sub
covers
.BR sub/deep/c.txt ;
and, for a
.I path
containing
.BR * ", " ? ", " [ " or " \e ,
a wildcard match in which
.B *
crosses
.BR / .
The last two do not combine \(em
.B \(dqo[tx]her\(dq
reports nothing even when
.B other/
has changes underneath, because the leading-directory rule applies only to
a literal
.IR path .
A trailing
.B /
asks for entries under the name, so
.B sub/
lists sub's contents while
.B a.txt/
matches nothing. Pathspec magic
.RB ( :(icase) ", " :! ", " :/ )
is rejected rather than taken literally.
.PP
.B --
separates revisions from paths. Without it, each argument is examined as git
examines it: an argument that is both a valid revision and an existing file
is refused as ambiguous, the first argument that names an existing path ends
the revision list, and every argument after that one must exist too. An
argument carrying a wildcard is taken as a path without being looked up on
disk.
.PP
The patch body matches git byte for byte in the great majority of cases,
including the
.B index
line, mode and
.B /dev/null
lines for additions and deletions, hunk splitting at three lines of context,
the function-name suffix and
.BR "\e No newline at end of file" .
Hunk positioning matches too: since the alignment step became a direct port
of git's own Myers algorithm, the two to three percent of hunks that used to
differ in position are gone, re-measured at zero mismatches over several
thousand randomized comparisons against real git.
.PP
.B \-\-histogram
aligns with git's histogram algorithm instead of the Myers default. This
matters because the two are used in different places by git itself: git
.B diff
defaults to Myers, while git
.B merge
defaults to histogram, and
.B sg
mirrors both, so
.B sg merge
uses histogram with no flag involved. git's other spellings
.RB ( --patience ,
.BR --minimal ,
.BR --diff-algorithm= <name> )
are not implemented and are rejected as unknown options rather than
approximated with one of the two aligners
.B sg
has.
.PP
A combinable unresolved conflict (both index stage 2 and stage 3 present,
plain index-vs-working-directory comparison only) is rendered as git's
combined diff, matching git byte for byte:
.B "diff --cc"
by default (dense, git's own PATCH default) or with
.BR --cc ,
.B "diff --combined"
with
.B -c
(every difference from either side, unpruned). The last of
.B -c
and
.B --cc
on the command line wins.
.PP
Given exactly one
.I rev
and no
.BR --cached ,
.B -c
and
.B --cc
instead render a combined diff of the index's own entry for each path and
that
.IR rev 's
blob, against the working-tree file. This applies to every path, not just
conflicted ones, and a path is combined only when all three of those sides
exist; otherwise its row falls back to exactly what plain
.BI "sg diff " rev
would have printed for it. Three consequences are easy to overlook: the
index entry used is the one with the lowest stage present, which for an
add/add conflict is stage 2 rather than stage 1; the output is reordered so
that every combined row precedes every non-combined one, each half in path
order; and rename and copy detection never sees a combined row.
.B --cached
and a second
.I rev
each override the flags entirely, leaving their ordinary output unchanged.
.B -c
and
.B --cc
also change the machine-readable formats for a combinable row: it collapses
to a single
.B MM
row in
.BR --name-status ,
the path once in
.BR --name-only ,
and disappears entirely from
.B --stat
/
.BR --numstat / --shortstat .
Without either flag, those five formats are unaffected by this change.
.PP
A path whose content is byte-identical to one that disappeared in the same
diff is reported as a rename, as git does by default:
.B R100
in
.BR --name-status ,
the destination alone in
.BR --name-only ,
and
.B "similarity index"
with
.B "rename from" / "rename to"
in a patch.
.B --stat
and
.B --numstat
compress the pair around its common directories, so a move from
.B a/b/c.txt
to
.B a/z/c.txt
prints as
.BR "a/{b => z}/c.txt" ;
a path needing quoting is printed in full instead, since quotes cannot
appear in the middle of a path.
.B --no-renames
turns detection off and
.BI -M <n>
sets the threshold. Both exact and inexact (similarity-scored) renames are
found; a move plus an edit is reported the same way git reports it, e.g.
.BR R093 .
.BI -C <n>
finds copies as well: a source that is still present (edited or untouched)
becomes eligible too, and may be paired more than once, each additional
destination reported as a copy rather than a rename.
.B --find-copies-harder
(equivalently, a second
.BR -C )
widens the source pool further to every path that is completely UNCHANGED
between the two sides being compared, at the cost of scanning every such
path. It composes with
.BI -C <n>
the way git's own does: the threshold from whichever
.B -M
/
.B -C
came last still applies, and
.B --no-renames
turns detection off without clearing
.BR --find-copies-harder 's
own effect \(em a later
.B -M
or
.B -C
is what actually cancels it. Both
.BI -M <n>
and
.BI -C <n>
accept
.I n
as either a whole percentage or, like git, a
.RB \(dq % \(dq -suffixed
percentage or a fraction, sharing the same grammar as
.BR "git diff" .
Detection runs after any
.I path
arguments have been applied, which is git's order: naming only one half of a
rename leaves nothing to pair with, and the half that remains is reported as
an ordinary addition or deletion. Renames and copies are not shown by
.BR "sg status" .
.SS branch
.B sg branch
.RB [ -d | --delete ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ name ]
.PP
With no arguments, lists every branch, one per line, with the current
branch marked by a leading
.BR * .
The listing merges the loose files under
.B refs/heads/
with the entries in
.BR packed-refs ,
so a branch that
.B "git gc"
has packed away is still listed; where both exist the loose value wins,
which is the same precedence
.B sg
uses when resolving a single ref.
.PP
With
.IR name ,
creates a branch pointing at the current
.B HEAD
commit and does
.B not
switch to it (use
.B "sg switch"
for that). Creating fails if the name already exists in either store, if
the repository has no commits yet, or if the name is not a valid ref name
\(em names may not begin with
.BR - ,
begin or end with
.BR / ,
end with
.B .
or
.BR .lock ,
contain
.BR // ,
.BR .. ,
.BR @{ ,
a backslash, whitespace, an ASCII control character, or any of
.BR ~^:?*[ ,
and may not be exactly
.BR HEAD .
Names containing
.B /
(for example
.BR feature/x )
are valid.
.PP
With
.B -d
(or
.BR --delete ),
deletes
.IR name .
Deletion removes the branch from
.B both
the loose file and
.BR packed-refs ;
removing only the loose file would leave a stale packed entry that
silently resurrects the branch at an older commit. The current branch is
never deletable, with or without
.BR --force .
.PP
If the branch is not fully merged into
.B HEAD
its commits would become unreachable, so deletion asks for confirmation
first; the prompt names the branch and its tip. As elsewhere in
.BR sg ,
.B --force
skips the prompt but not the refusal to delete the current branch, and a
non-interactive standard input refuses rather than assuming yes (see
.BR "EXIT STATUS" ).
A successful deletion prints the deleted tip's full object id \(em because
.B "sg undo"
snapshots the working tree and index but not refs, that printed id is the
only way to recreate the branch.
.SS tag
.B sg tag
.RB [ -a ]
.RB [ -m
.IR msg ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ -- ]
.RI [ name
.RI [ rev ]]
.br
.B sg tag
.RB [ -d | --delete ]
.I name
.PP
With no arguments, lists every tag under
.BR refs/tags/ ,
one per line, sorted byte-wise. As with
.BR "sg branch" ,
the listing merges the loose files with
.B packed-refs
entries, so a tag
.B "git gc"
has packed away is still listed.
.PP
With
.IR name ,
creates a tag named
.I name
pointing at
.I rev
(a commit, branch, or tag name, or a full 40-hex object id; see
.B REVISIONS
below), defaulting to
.B HEAD
if
.I rev
is omitted. Creating fails if a tag by that name already exists, unless
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
is given, in which case the existing tag is overwritten; it also fails if
.I name
is not a valid ref name (the same rules
.B "sg branch"
enforces).
.PP
Without
.B -a
or
.BR -m ,
the tag is
.IR lightweight :
.B refs/tags/\fIname\fR
is written directly as a ref to the target commit, no tag object is
created. With
.B -a
(annotated) or
.B -m
.I msg
(which implies
.BR -a ),
.B sg
instead builds a
.B tag
object \(em recording the target object, its type, the tag name, a tagger
identity/timestamp, and the message \(em writes it as a loose object, and
points
.B refs/tags/\fIname\fR
at that tag object's id instead of the target directly. The message is
normalized the same way as
.BR "sg commit" 's
(real git's default
.BR --cleanup=whitespace ),
but unlike
.BR "sg commit" ,
an empty (or all-whitespace) message is
.I not
rejected \(em it produces a tag object with an empty message segment,
matching real git's own
.B "git tag -a -m ''"
behavior, which is intentionally asymmetric with
.BR "git commit -m ''" .
.B -a
without
.B -m
is a usage error;
.B sg
has no editor integration to prompt for a message (same restriction as
.BR "sg commit" ).
The tagger identity is read from
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.BR GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL ,
defaulting to
.B small_git
/
.BR sg@localhost ,
with a fixed
.B +0000
timezone offset.
.PP
With
.B -d
(or
.BR --delete ),
deletes
.IR name .
As with branch deletion, this removes the tag from
.B both
the loose file and
.B packed-refs
so a stale packed entry cannot resurrect it; deleting a tag that does not
exist is an error, not a silent success. Unlike branch deletion there is no
merge check or confirmation prompt \(em a tag is just a name for an object,
not a line of unique history that could become unreachable.
.B -d
combined with any tag-creation option
.RB ( -a ", " -m ", " --force / -f )
or a second name argument is a usage error, matching real git; this project
does not support deleting more than one tag per invocation, but rejects the
attempt rather than silently deleting only the first name.
.PP
.B sg push
can also transmit tags; see
.B push
below for
.BR --tags .
.SS reset
.B sg reset
.RB [ --soft | --mixed | --hard ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RI [ rev ]
.PP
Moves the current branch to
.I rev
(defaulting to
.BR HEAD ,
so a bare
.B sg reset
re-points the branch at its own current commit \(em a no-op for the branch
itself, but see the index effects of
.B --mixed
below). On a detached
.B HEAD
it moves
.B HEAD
itself and leaves every branch alone, matching real git. This is also what
makes it usable while a rebase is paused, since a paused rebase is a detached
.BR HEAD .
A
.B HEAD
that is neither a branch nor a commit id is corrupt, and is refused. Unlike
.BR "sg restore" ,
.B reset
has no pathspec form; passing paths after
.B --
is rejected with a pointer to
.B "sg restore --staged"
instead.
.PP
The three modes differ only in how much beyond the branch ref they touch,
and that difference drives three different safety postures:
.TP
.B --soft
Moves only the branch ref.
The index and working directory are left exactly as they are, so nothing
uncommitted is ever at risk \(em there is no confirmation prompt and no
automatic snapshot, matching real git.
.B --soft
refuses outright (exit 1, no changes made) if a merge is unresolved or a
rebase is paused, since silently abandoning that state out from under a
soft reset would be surprising and unrecoverable through
.B --soft
itself; resolve or abort the merge/rebase first.
.TP
.B --mixed
(the default mode when no
.BR --soft / --hard
flag is given) additionally rebuilds the index from
.IR rev 's
tree, discarding whatever was staged. That can lose uncommitted staged
work, but never touches the working directory, so \(em like real git \(em
there is still no interactive confirmation; instead
.B sg
takes an automatic safety snapshot (recoverable via
.BR "sg undo" )
before rewriting the index. If a merge is in progress,
.B --mixed
also clears
.BR MERGE_HEAD ,
ending the merge (matching real git: a later, unrelated
.B "sg commit"
must not silently record a bogus merge commit for a merge the user just
abandoned via reset). It deliberately does
.B not
touch an in-progress rebase's sequencer state \(em matching real git, which
also leaves a paused rebase's on-disk state alone here. No mode of
.B reset
ends a rebase; only
.BR "sg rebase --abort" ,
.BR "sg rebase --quit" ,
a run that completes, or
.B "sg undo"
does.
.TP
.B --hard
Additionally overwrites the working directory to match
.IR rev 's
tree, via the same
.B sg_safe_apply_tree
machinery
.B "sg switch"
and
.B "sg restore"
use: an interactive confirmation prompt (bypassable with
.B --force
/
.BR -f ,
real git has no equivalent flag \(em this project's own addition) followed
by an automatic safety snapshot, since this is the one mode that can
overwrite uncommitted working-directory changes outright.
.IP
A paused rebase's sequencer state survives
.BR --hard ,
exactly as it survives
.BR --mixed ,
matching real git: only
.BR rebase 's
own subcommands end a rebase. A conflicted merge's
.B MERGE_HEAD
is cleared, also matching real git.
.PP
Passing more than one of
.BR --soft / --mixed / --hard
is a usage error (exit 1) \(em a deliberate divergence from real git, which
accepts the combination and lets the last flag silently win. An ambiguous
invocation is rejected here rather than guessed at.
.SS switch
.B sg switch
.RB [ -c ]
.RB [ --detach ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I branch
.PP
Switches the current branch to
.IR branch ,
updating the working directory and index to match its tip commit.
.B -c
creates
.I branch
(from the current
.BR HEAD )
before switching to it; it is an error if a branch by that name already
exists, or if the current branch has no commits yet.
.PP
.B --detach
points
.B HEAD
directly at a commit instead of at a branch, and accepts any revision rather
than only a branch name. Without it, naming something that resolves to a
commit but is not a branch is refused with a pointer to this flag \(em real
.B "git switch"
refuses there too rather than detaching silently.
.B -c
and
.B --detach
together are a usage error: they ask for two different
.BR HEAD s.
Leaving a detached
.B HEAD
prints \(dqPrevious HEAD position was ...\(dq first, but only when the commit
actually changes.
.PP
While detached,
.BR "sg commit" ,
.BR "sg reset" ,
.BR "sg branch" ,
.BR "sg stash" ,
.B "sg log"
and
.B "sg status"
all work, and the state is reported the way real git reports it
(\(dqHEAD detached at <id>\(dq, becoming \(dqfrom\(dq once
.B HEAD
moves off the commit it was detached at).
.B "sg merge"
and starting a
.B "sg rebase"
are refused, a deliberate limit rather than a technical one.
.PP
While a rebase is paused on a conflict,
.B switch
is refused outright \(em including with
.BR --force ,
which cannot override it. Finish the rebase with
.B "sg rebase --continue"
or end it with
.BR "sg rebase --abort" .
This matches real git, whose
.B "git switch"
likewise refuses (\(dqcannot switch branch while rebasing\(dq) rather than
clobbering the sequencer state, and leaves the new branch un-created when
.B -c
was given.
.PP
An in-progress merge is refused the same way. Once
.B "sg merge"
has stopped on a conflict,
.B switch
is refused until the merge is concluded \(em finish it with
.B "sg commit"
or end it with
.BR "sg merge --abort" .
.B --force
does not override this either, and
.B -c
leaves the new branch un-created. The refusal keys on the presence of
.BR MERGE_HEAD ,
not on the index still holding conflicted stages: resolving every conflict
and staging the result still leaves the merge in progress, and
.B switch
still refuses. Real git behaves identically (\(dqcannot switch branch while
merging\(dq); note that real git's
.B "git checkout -f"
does allow it, but
.B sg
has no
.B checkout
subcommand and follows
.BR "git switch" .
.PP
Otherwise, if switching would discard uncommitted changes (staged or
unstaged), the affected paths are listed and confirmation is required
before proceeding (an automatic safety snapshot is taken first \(em see
.B undo
below); pass
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
to skip the interactive confirmation. A non-interactive stdin (e.g. in a
script) is always treated as \(dqno\(dq unless
.B --force
is given.
.SS restore
.B sg restore
.RB [ --staged ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I path
.RI [ path ...]
.PP
Restores one or more paths. With
.BR --staged ,
unstages
.I path
(resets its index entry to match
.B HEAD
if tracked there, or removes it from the index otherwise) without touching
the working directory. Without
.BR --staged ,
overwrites
.IR path 's
working-directory content with what is currently staged in the index
(not
.BR HEAD ).
.PP
If restoring without
.B --staged
would discard working-directory content that differs from the index, the
affected paths are listed and confirmation is required (with an automatic
safety snapshot taken first), same as
.BR switch .
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
skips the interactive confirmation.
.SS undo
.B sg undo
.RI [ N ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.PP
Without
.IR N ,
lists the repository's automatic safety snapshots (newest first, numbered),
each with its timestamp and a short label describing what created it (e.g.
a
.BR switch ", " restore ", " merge ", or " rebase
that overwrote something). With
.IR N ,
restores the working directory and index to snapshot number
.IR N .
Restoring goes through the same dirty-working-tree confirmation and
automatic snapshot as
.B switch
(so an
.B undo
can itself be undone);
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
skips the confirmation prompt.
.PP
Restoring a snapshot while a rebase is paused
.I does
abandon that rebase's sequencer state, and says so on standard error. This
is the one place
.B sg
deliberately ends a rebase from outside
.BR rebase 's
own subcommands: unlike
.BR switch " and " reset --hard ,
.B undo
has no real-git equivalent to take behaviour from, and resuming a rebase on
top of a working tree that
.B undo
just rewound out from under it would not be meaningful.
.PP
Snapshots are created automatically by
.BR switch ,
.BR restore ,
.BR merge ,
and
.B rebase
whenever they are about to overwrite uncommitted work; there is no manual
\(dqtake a snapshot now\(dq command.
.B switch
never snapshots its way past an in-progress merge or a paused rebase \(em
it refuses both outright (see
.B switch
above). See
.B FILES
for where snapshots are stored.
.SS repack
.B sg repack
.PP
Packs every loose object currently under
.B objects/
into a single new packfile, then removes the now-redundant loose copies.
If there are no loose objects, prints a message and does nothing. Takes no
arguments or flags; there is no delta compression tuning, no
.BR "--window" / "--depth" ,
and no garbage collection of unreachable objects.
.SS merge
.B sg merge
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.I branch
.br
.B sg merge --abort
.PP
Merges
.I branch
into whatever
.B HEAD
names. On a detached
.B HEAD
that is
.B HEAD
itself: no branch ref moves, the single reflog line goes to
.B logs/HEAD
alone, and the generated merge message and conflict markers say
.B HEAD
where they would otherwise name the branch \(em all matching real git. If
what
.B HEAD
names is a strict ancestor of
.I branch
(or has no commits yet), this is a fast-forward. Otherwise a three-way
merge is attempted using the merge base; conflicting files are written to
the working directory with conflict markers and left staged at stages 1/2/3
for resolution (resolve with
.BR "sg add <file>..." ,
then
.BR "sg commit" ).
.PP
Refuses to start if the working directory is not clean, or if a merge or
rebase is already in progress. A fast-forward or the start of a three-way
merge takes an automatic safety snapshot first (see
.BR undo );
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
only affects the interactive confirmation for a dirty-tree fast-forward, not
whether a snapshot is taken.
.PP
.B --abort
cancels an in-progress merge, restoring the working directory/index to the
pre-merge
.B HEAD
and removing
.BR MERGE_HEAD ,
also taking an automatic safety snapshot first. It only needs
.B MERGE_HEAD
to exist, not to be readable: a corrupt one is exactly the state most in
need of aborting, and it is cleared here (and by
.BR "reset --hard" / --mixed
and
.BR "stash push" )
just as real git clears it.
.SS merge-base
.B sg merge-base
.I commit-a commit-b
.PP
Prints the hex id of the best common ancestor of the two given commits
(each given as a full 40-character hex object id, not a ref name). If
multiple, mutually unreachable common ancestors exist (a criss-cross merge
history), this is reported as an error rather than picking one arbitrarily.
.SS rebase
.B sg rebase
.I upstream
.br
.B sg rebase --continue
.br
.B sg rebase --skip
.br
.B sg rebase --abort
.br
.B sg rebase --quit
.PP
Replays the commits unique to the current branch (since its merge base with
.IR upstream )
one at a time on top of
.IR upstream 's
tip, then moves the current branch to point at the result. Exactly one of
.IR upstream ,
.BR --continue ,
.BR --skip ,
.BR --abort ,
or
.B --quit
must be given.
.PP
If
.I upstream
is already an ancestor of the current branch, nothing is replayed
(\(dqup to date\(dq). If the current branch is an ancestor of
.IR upstream ,
this is a pure fast-forward. Rebasing a range that includes a merge commit
is not supported and is reported as an error, with a suggestion to use
.B merge
instead. A commit that becomes empty after replay (its change is already
present upstream) is silently skipped rather than recorded.
.PP
When a replayed commit conflicts, the rebase pauses with conflict markers
and stage 1/2/3 index entries, the same shape as a
.B merge
conflict; resolve with
.BR "sg add <file>..." ,
then
.BR "sg rebase --continue" .
.B --skip
discards the conflicting commit entirely and continues with the next one.
.B --abort
restores the original branch tip and working directory, discarding all
progress made so far.
.B --quit
removes the paused state and touches nothing else \(em it does not move
.BR HEAD ,
does not return to the original branch, and does not touch the index or
working directory, leaving a conflicted path exactly as it was.
Unlike
.BR --continue ,
.BR --skip ,
and
.BR --abort ,
which all require the state under
.B .git/sg-rebase/
to parse and refuse identically to real git when it does not,
.B --quit
parses nothing and is the only one of the four guaranteed to work on a
damaged state; use it as the escape hatch when the other three refuse.
.PP
Rebase state is stored under
.B .git/sg-rebase/
(see
.BR FILES );
.B sg status
reports an in-progress rebase and the commands above are the only supported
way to leave that state. Requires the current branch (\c
.BR HEAD )
to be readable, but it need not be on a branch: started from a detached
.BR HEAD ,
the replay happens exactly as it always does and simply has no branch to move
at the end, so no branch ref and no branch reflog is touched and
.B logs/HEAD
ends at the last replayed commit with no
.B rebase (finish)
line \(em matching real git. Refuses to start on top of an in-progress merge,
and refuses ordinary
.B commit
while a rebase is in progress.
.SS cherry-pick
.B sg cherry-pick
.RB [ -n | --no-commit ]
.RB [ -m
.IR parent-number ]
.I commit\fR...
.br
.B sg cherry-pick --continue
.br
.B sg cherry-pick --skip
.br
.B sg cherry-pick --abort
.br
.B sg cherry-pick --quit
.PP
Replays each
.I commit
in the order given, one at a time, on top of whatever
.B HEAD
names (the current branch, or
.B HEAD
itself when detached), the same three-way merge as
.BR merge / rebase
with
.IR commit \(cq s
own parent tree as the base. The new commit's author (name, email, date) is
copied verbatim from the picked commit; only the committer reflects
here-and-now. For a
.I commit
with more than one parent,
.B -m
.I parent-number
selects which parent (1-based) to diff against; without it a merge commit
is refused.
.B -n
(or
.BR --no-commit )
stages the result without committing, and is only accepted with exactly one
.IR commit .
.B --no-edit
is accepted as a no-op (\c
.B sg
never opens an editor); \c
.BR -e / --edit
and every other option real git's cherry-pick has that
.B sg
does not implement
.RB ( -x ", " -s / --signoff ", " -S ", " --ff ", " --allow-empty ", "
.BR --allow-empty-message ", " --keep-redundant-commits ", " --empty= ", "
.BR --cleanup= ", " --strategy ", " -X ", " --rerere-autoupdate ", " --reference )
are rejected with the usage line rather than silently ignored or
approximated. A range argument
.RI ( A..B )
is likewise rejected, not expanded.
.PP
A conflicting or already-applied (empty) pick pauses the whole sequence:
conflict markers and stage 1/2/3 index entries are left for the former,
nothing is written for the latter beyond the paused state, and in both
cases
.B .git/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD
plus
.B .git/MERGE_MSG
are written (see
.BR FILES ).
Picking more than one commit additionally records the remaining sequence
under
.BR .git/sequencer/ ,
in the exact layout and file names real git uses \(em unlike
.BR .git/sg-rebase/ ,
which is deliberately incompatible, this state is meant to be readable (and
finishable) by a real
.B git
binary too. Resolve a conflict with
.BR "sg add <file>..." ,
then
.BR "sg cherry-pick --continue" ;
.B --skip
takes an automatic safety snapshot, discards the paused commit, and
continues with the rest;
.B --abort
takes a safety snapshot, restores the working directory and
.B HEAD
(or the current branch) to their state before the whole sequence started,
and refuses if
.B HEAD
has moved since the pick stopped;
.B --quit
removes the paused state and touches nothing else, leaving the conflicted
index and working directory exactly as they were.
.PP
Refuses to start if the working directory is not clean, or if a merge,
rebase, or another cherry-pick/revert is already in progress. Ordinary
.B sg commit
is refused while a cherry-pick is paused (real git allows it and finishes
the pick instead \(em a deliberate divergence, see
.BR CAVEATS );
use
.B --continue
instead.
.SS revert
.B sg revert
.RB [ -n | --no-commit ]
.RB [ -m
.IR parent-number ]
.RB [ --no-edit ]
.I commit\fR...
.br
.B sg revert --continue
.br
.B sg revert --skip
.br
.B sg revert --abort
.br
.B sg revert --quit
.PP
Applies the inverse of each
.I commit
in the order given, one at a time, on top of whatever
.B HEAD
names \(em the same replay engine as
.BR cherry-pick ,
with the base and target trees swapped: the picked commit's own tree is the
merge base, and its parent's tree is what
.B HEAD
is merged against. Unlike
.BR cherry-pick ,
the new commit's author
.I and
committer both come from the environment/here-and-now (real git authors a
revert as the current user, it does not copy the reverted commit's
author).
.B -m
.I parent-number
selects a merge's parent the same way
.B cherry-pick
does; without it a merge commit is refused.
.B -n
(or
.BR --no-commit )
stages the result without committing, and is only accepted with exactly one
.IR commit .
.B --no-edit
is accepted as a no-op
.RB ( sg
never opens an editor to let the generated message be changed); every
option
.B cherry-pick
does not implement is rejected here too, for the same reason.
.PP
The generated commit message follows git's own wording exactly: a plain
revert wraps the reverted commit's subject as
.RB \(dq Revert\ \(dq \fIsubject\fP \(dq ,
followed by a blank line and
.RB \(dq "This\ reverts\ commit" \ \fIid\fP\(dq .
Reverting with
.B -m
adds
.RB \(dq ,\ reversing\c
\fR\(rsn\fPchanges\ made\ to\c
\fR \fIparent-id\fP\(dq
after the id \(em the line break there is literal, not wrapping. If the
subject being reverted already begins with the exact eight bytes
.RB \(dq Revert\ \(rq\c
\fR\(dq
(no closing-quote or balance check), that prefix is replaced with
.RB \(dq Reapply\ \(rq\c
\fR\(dq
and the rest of the subject is kept verbatim, matching git's own
already-reverted-a-revert wording.
.PP
Conflict/empty-pick pausing, the
.B .git/sequencer/
state for a multi-commit sequence, and
.BR --continue / --skip / --abort / --quit
all work exactly as documented under
.BR cherry-pick ,
substituting
.B .git/REVERT_HEAD
for
.BR .git/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD .
One wording differs: a direct revert's reflog entry reads
.RB \(dq revert:\ \(dq\c
\fIsubject\fP, but a revert resumed via
.B --continue
logs a bare
.RB \(dq commit:\ \(dq\c
\fIsubject\fP with
.I no
.RB \(dq (cherry-pick) \(dq -style
suffix \(em both wordings were measured against real git, and the
asymmetry is intentional, not a naming inconsistency between the two
commands.
.PP
Refuses to start if the working directory is not clean, or if a merge,
rebase, or another cherry-pick/revert is already in progress. Ordinary
.B sg commit
is refused while a revert is paused, for the same reason and with the same
divergence from real git as
.BR cherry-pick ;
use
.B --continue
instead.
.SS clone
.B sg clone
.I url
.RI [ directory ]
.PP
Clones a repository served over the git smart HTTP protocol or reached over
SSH (see
.B TRANSPORTS
below) (an
.B http://
or
.B https://
URL) into a new directory. If
.I directory
is omitted, it is derived from the last path segment of
.IR url ,
with a trailing
.B .git
stripped. Refuses to clone into an existing, non-empty directory.
.PP
After fetching, checks out the remote's default branch (preferring its
advertised HEAD symref, then
.BR main ,
then
.BR master ,
then the first advertised branch) and records
.BR "[remote \(dqorigin\(dq]"
and a tracking
.B [branch]
stanza in
.BR .git/config .
If the remote has no branches (only tags, or is entirely empty), the clone
completes without a checkout.
.PP
Also creates
.BR refs/remotes/origin/HEAD ,
a
.B symbolic
ref pointing at the remote's default branch, with a single
.B "clone: from " \fIurl\fP
line in its reflog \(em matching real git. The remote-tracking
.B branches
get no reflog at all at clone time; theirs begin at the first
.BR fetch ,
which is also what real git does.
.PP
There is no support for
.B git://
or local filesystem clone sources, and no
.BR --depth / --branch / --bare / --origin
options; the remote is always named
.BR origin .
.SS fetch
.B sg fetch
.RI [ remote ]
.PP
Fetches new commits and refs from
.I remote
(default
.BR origin ,
which must already have a
.B url
configured, e.g. by
.BR clone ).
Remote branches are written to
.BR refs/remotes/ \fIremote\fP / ,
tags are written directly to
.BR refs/tags/ .
Neither the current branch nor
.B HEAD
is changed. Have-negotiation is a simplified, capped ancestry walk (not a
full git protocol v2 negotiation) \(em this can only cause some redundant
data to be transferred, never an incorrect result. Takes no other flags;
there is no
.BR --all / --prune / --tags-only .
.SS push
.B sg push
.RI [ remote ]
.RI [ refspec ... ]
.RB [ --tags ]
.RB [ --force | -f ]
.RB [ --delete
.IR name ... ]
.PP
A
.I refspec
may be
.RI [ + ] src [: dst ],
a wildcard pattern such as
.BR refs/heads/*:refs/heads/* ,
or the bare push-matching
.RB [ + ] : .
.PP
Pushes to
.I remote
(default
.BR origin )
over the git smart HTTP protocol or over SSH (see
.B TRANSPORTS
below). With no
.I refspec
and no
.BR --tags ,
pushes the current branch (and a name is required if
.B HEAD
is detached).
.PP
Each
.I refspec
has the form
.BI [+] src [: dst ],
matched literally against real git 2.55.0's own grammar:
.IP \(bu 3
.I src
is resolved through the full revision grammar (branch, tag, full 40-hex,
.BR HEAD ,
.BR ~N / ^N / @{N} ,
etc.), evaluated locally before any connection is made. If ANY
.I src
in the whole command fails to resolve, the entire push aborts before
connecting \(em not one ref lands, matching real git's own behavior for a
multi-refspec push with one bad
.IR src .
An annotated tag given as
.I src
is pushed as the tag object itself, not peeled to the commit it points at.
.IP \(bu 3
.I dst
(after the local ref is resolved but before the actual update) is completed
against the remote's advertisement: if it already starts with
.BR refs/ ,
it is used as-is; otherwise, if the remote already has a ref of that exact
name, that ref is updated \(em and if
.I dst
matches
.B more than one
advertised ref (say both a branch and a tag of that short name), the push is
rejected outright rather than guessing which was meant
.RI ( "dst refspec '" dst "' matches more than one" ),
leaving every ref on the remote untouched; otherwise, if
.I src
is itself under
.B refs/heads/
or
.BR refs/tags/ ,
that same prefix is prepended to
.I dst
in full. If none of these apply, the push is rejected
.RI ( "The destination you provided is not a full refname..." ) .
When multiple refspecs are given, a
.I dst
that cannot be completed this way aborts the whole batch, the same as an
unresolvable
.IR src .
.IP \(bu 3
Omitting
.I dst
(bare
.IR src )
falls back to the pre-refspec behavior described below: an explicit name is
looked up as both a branch and a tag, and if both exist the push is
rejected as ambiguous
.RI ( "src refspec '" name "' matches more than one" ) .
.IP \(bu 3
.BI : dst
(empty
.IR src )
deletes
.I dst
on the remote, equivalent to
.BI --delete\  dst
below.
.I src:
(empty
.IR dst )
is a syntax error.
.IP \(bu 3
A leading
.B +
forces that one refspec only, regardless of
.BR --force .
.PP
.B --force
(or
.BR -f )
forces every refspec in the command, allowing a non-fast-forward update of
a pushed branch; without it (and without a
.B +
prefix on that refspec), a branch push that is not a fast-forward is
rejected. A tag update is never treated as a fast-forward or merge question
at all: if the remote already has a tag of that name pointing at a
different object, the push of that tag is rejected outright
.RI ( "tag '" name "' already exists on the remote (use --force to overwrite)" )
unless forced, in which case the remote tag is overwritten. With multiple
refspecs (including
.BR --tags ),
a rejected ref does not stop the others from being pushed \(em that
rejection is per-ref, unlike an unresolvable
.I src
or an uncompletable
.I dst
above, both of which abort the whole batch \(em but the command still exits
non-zero if any ref was rejected.
.PP
.B --tags
pushes every local tag under
.B refs/tags/
in one round trip, instead of a branch, and cannot be combined with an
explicit refspec. Pushing a tag never creates a
.B refs/remotes/<remote>/<tag>
tracking ref (real git does not either); that only ever happens for a
pushed branch.
.PP
.BI --delete\  name ...
deletes one or more named refs on the remote (each may be a short name or a
full
.B refs/...
path) without pushing anything. It is rejected outright, before
connecting, if any
.I name
contains a
.B :
.RI ( "--delete only accepts plain target ref names" ) .
Deleting a ref the remote does not have is a per-ref failure
.RI ( "unable to delete '" name "': remote ref does not exist" ) ,
it does not stop other names in the same
.B --delete
invocation from being processed. Deleting a ref does not remove the local
.B refs/remotes/<remote>/<name>
tracking ref, if one exists.
.PP
A wildcard refspec (a pattern carrying exactly one
.B *
on each side) pushes every LOCAL ref the source pattern matches, creating
remote refs that do not exist yet; whatever the star stood for is
substituted into the destination pattern. The star may sit anywhere,
including mid-segment, and it crosses
.BR / .
Written without a colon, a pattern mirrors itself. A pattern matching
nothing is not an error. An annotated tag matched by a pattern is pushed as
the tag object, unpeeled, and the expanded destination is used verbatim \(em
it is
.I not
completed against the advertisement the way an explicit
.I dst
is. A star on only one side, two stars on a side, and a wildcard deletion
are each rejected as an invalid refspec, the last because approximating it
would delete every matching remote ref.
.PP
The bare push-matching refspec
.RB ( :
or
.BR +: )
pushes every local branch that
.I already exists
on the remote. It never creates a remote ref and never touches tags; a
local-only branch is left alone. Per-ref rules are unchanged, so one branch
can land while another is refused as a non-fast-forward.
.PP
Exit codes are 0 or 1 only, never a third value: this
differs from real git, which exits 128 for a client-side refspec syntax
error (empty
.IR dst ,
a colon in a
.B --delete
name) \(em sg reports the identical situation with exit 1, matching this
project's own exit-code convention.
.PP
If this repository has ever used chunked storage locally, the push also
attempts to update the remote's
.B refs/sg/chunks
keep-alive ref alongside whatever refs are being pushed (atomically, if the
server advertises that capability) so chunk blobs stay reachable on the
remote too; see
.B FILES
below.
.SS chunk-info
.B sg chunk-info
.IR "path\-or\-blob\-hex"
.PP
Prints diagnostic information about how a file (given as a working-tree
path currently in the index, or as a full 40-character blob hex id) is
stored: whether it is chunked, its original size, its chunk count, and (if
chunked) how many of its chunks are also referenced by some other chunked
blob in the repository (\(dqshared chunks\(dq). This is a read-only
diagnostic command; it does not modify anything.
.SS Path quoting in output
.PP
Wherever
.B sg
prints a path \(em
.BR status ,
.BR diff ,
.B cat\-file
on a tree, and its own diagnostics \(em a path containing a byte that would
otherwise reach the terminal unchanged is wrapped in double quotes with
C\-style escapes, exactly as
.B git
does it: \(rs\(rs and \(rs" for a backslash and a double quote, the named
forms \(rsa \(rsb \(rst \(rsn \(rsv \(rsf \(rsr, and a zero\-padded
three\-digit octal escape such as \(rs033 for every other byte below 0x20 and
for DEL. A space is never escaped and never causes quoting on its own, so an
ordinary name containing one is printed bare.
.PP
Bytes at or above 0x80 are printed as they are, which is
.B git
run with
.BR core.quotepath=false ,
not its default. A path in a non\-ASCII script therefore appears as itself
rather than as octal escapes.
.B sg
has no configuration file, so this is fixed rather than a setting; pass
.B \-c core.quotepath=false
on the
.B git
side when comparing the two directly.
.SS stash
.B sg stash
.RB [ push ]
.RB [ -m
.IR message ]
.RB [ -u | \-\-include\-untracked ]
.RB [ -a | \-\-all ]
.RB [ \-\-keep\-index ]
.br
.B sg stash list
.br
.B sg stash show
.RB [ -p | \-\-patch ]
[\fB\-\-stat\fR[=\fIw\fR[,\fIn\fR]]]
.RB [ \-\-numstat ]
.RB [ \-\-shortstat ]
.RB [ \-\-name\-only ]
.RB [ \-\-name\-status ]
.RB [ -M [ <n> ]| -C [ <n> ]| \-\-find\-copies\-harder | \-\-no\-renames ]
.RB [ \-\-histogram ]
.RB [ -u | \-\-include\-untracked ]
.RB [ \-\-only\-untracked ]
.RI [ stash ]
.br
.B sg stash apply
.RB [ \-\-index ]
.RI [ stash ]
.br
.B sg stash pop
.RB [ \-\-index ]
.RI [ stash ]
.br
.B sg stash drop
.RI [ stash ]
.br
.B sg stash clear
.PP
Saves the current index and working-directory changes away, resetting both
back to
.BR HEAD ,
so they can be restored later. Backed by a real reflog file at
.BR .git/logs/refs/stash ,
the same on-disk mechanism real git uses \(em a repository stashed with
.B sg
is fully readable by
.B git stash list / show / pop
and vice versa; see
.B FILES
below.
.PP
The reset that
.B "sg stash push"
performs is itself logged, as real git logs it: one
.B "reset: moving to HEAD"
line in
.BR .git/logs/HEAD .
The current branch's own log gets nothing \(em the reset moves HEAD from a
commit to the same commit, and a concrete ref's log suppresses a no-op while
.B logs/HEAD
does not.
.PP
.I stash
identifies an entry as
.BR stash@{ N } ,
a bare
.IR N
(equivalent to
.BR stash@{ N } ),
or, if omitted, the most recent entry
.RB ( stash@{0} ).
.PP
With no subcommand, or with an explicit
.BR push ,
saves the change and reports
.RB \(dq "No local changes to save" \(dq
(exit 0, nothing written) if the working tree and index already match
.BR HEAD
(and, under
.BR -u / -a ,
there is also no untracked file to save).
.B -m
.I message
sets the entry's description to \(dqOn
.IR branch : message\(dq;
without it, the description is the \(dqWIP on
.IR branch : short-hash
subject\(dq form real git uses. A conflicted (unmerged) index refuses the
push outright.
.PP
A tracked file deleted from the working directory is a change like any
other: the stash's own tree omits the path, so
.B pop
re-deletes it rather than bringing it back, and a deletion on its own is
enough to make a stash (it is not \(dqno local changes\(dq). The stash's
index parent still lists the file unless the deletion was staged. Popping a
deletion also removes any directory it leaves empty, up to but not including
the repository root; unlike the untracked sweep below, this ignores
.B .gitignore
entirely \(em a now-empty directory goes even if it is ignored, matching
real git 2.55.0.
.PP
A path whose name would resolve inside the repository's own
.B .git
directory is refused wherever it could reach the filesystem \(em checking
out a commit that carries such a tree entry, adding one, or restoring one
from the index. The comparison covers case variants and the trailing-dot
and trailing-space forms, and names that differ from
.B .git
only by code points a case-folding filesystem ignores. Ordinary names that
merely resemble it, such as
.BR .gitignore ,
.B .gitmodules
or
.BR git~1 ,
are unaffected.
.PP
By default untracked files are never stashed and are left untouched.
.B -u
(or
.BR \-\-include\-untracked )
also stashes them, into a third parent commit of the stash (a root commit
holding only the untracked files) so the tracked half's tree stays
byte-for-byte identical to a stash made without the flag (measured against
real git 2.55.0).
.B -a
(or
.BR \-\-all )
does the same but also sweeps up files that are
.BR .gitignore d.
Once anything is being stashed, this third parent is written
unconditionally, even when the untracked list is empty. After stashing,
.B -u
removes the taken files and prunes any directory left empty by their
removal, except a directory that still holds only ignored files, which
.B -u
leaves alone;
.B -a
removes those too.
.B \-\-keep\-index
resets the working tree to the
.B index
instead of
.BR HEAD ,
leaving whatever was staged still staged.
.PP
An in-progress rebase does
.B not
block
.BR push ,
matching real git \(em but resetting the working tree and index back to
.B HEAD
while a rebase is paused can make a later
.B "sg rebase --continue"
decide the paused commit's change already exists upstream and silently
skip it (measured identical to real git 2.55.0: the behavior itself is
intentional and left alone).
.B sg
prints an extra warning to standard error when this situation is detected;
real git does not warn, but the resulting state is the same on both.
A
.B MERGE_HEAD
left over from an in-progress merge is always removed by a successful
push, with an advisory line on standard error (real git removes it
silently).
.PP
.B show
prints a diff of the stashed changes without applying them, defaulting to a
diffstat
.RB ( \-\-stat ) ,
.I not
a patch \(em matching real git. Every format flag from
.B "sg diff"
is accepted
.RB ( -p | \-\-patch ,
.BR \-\-stat ,
.BR \-\-numstat ,
.BR \-\-shortstat ,
.BR \-\-name\-only ,
.BR \-\-name\-status ),
and so are the diff options
.RB ( -M [ <n> ],
.BR \-\-find\-renames [= <n> ],
.BR \-\-no\-renames ,
.RB -C [ <n> ],
.BR \-\-find\-copies [= <n> ],
.BR \-\-find\-copies\-harder ,
.BR \-\-histogram ).
.PP
Giving a diff option that is not itself a format selector switches the
output to a patch, exactly as if
.B -p
had been given \(em this is git's own rule, measured:
.B "git stash show -M"
prints a patch, not a stat. The stash-specific flags do
.I not
do this
.RB ( -u ,
.BR \-\-include\-untracked ,
.B \-\-only\-untracked
stay on the diffstat), and an explicit format wins regardless of which side
of the diff option it was written on, so
.B "-M \-\-stat"
and
.B "\-\-stat -M"
both print a stat.
.PP
The diff is rendered by the same code as
.BR "sg diff" .
.B \-\-stat
also takes real git's
.B \-\-stat=w[,n]
width\-limit suffix, same as
.BR "sg diff" .
By default the comparison is the stash's tracked half (the commit made at
push time) against its first parent (the tree at push time, before
.BR push ).
.B -u
/
.B \-\-include\-untracked
additionally includes a stash's third-parent untracked files, merged into
the same path-sorted list as an addition each; on a stash that has no
third parent it falls back to the tracked-only diff instead of erroring.
.B \-\-only\-untracked
shows only the untracked half (as if diffed against nothing); on a stash
with no third parent it prints nothing and exits 0. These two flags are a
single selector, not independent toggles \(em whichever is named last on
the command line wins (measured against real git 2.55.0).
.PP
.B apply
and
.B pop
three-way merge the stashed entry back into the working tree and index,
using conflict markers labelled \(dqUpdated upstream\(dq / \(dqStashed
changes\(dq (matching real git's literal wording, not the current branch
names).
.B pop
additionally drops the entry once the merge succeeds without conflict;
.B apply
leaves it on the stack either way. On conflict, the stash entry is
.I never
dropped and the working tree and index are left with stages 1/2/3 for the
conflicted paths, same as a conflicted
.BR merge .
.PP
.B apply
and
.B pop
no longer require the whole working directory and index to be clean; only a
path the merge actually touches may not be dirty. A touched path is refused
if the working tree has it and its content differs from
.BR HEAD
(even if that content happens to already equal what the stash would write),
or if the index already differs from
.BR HEAD
there; a touched path simply missing from the working tree is not a
collision, since nothing there could be overwritten. This is a deliberate
divergence from real git on one row: real git's \(dqours\(dq side is the
index, so it three-way-merges a staged change against the stash and may
report a conflict; sg's \(dqours\(dq is
.BR HEAD ,
so letting a staged change through here would silently discard it, and sg
refuses instead. An in-progress rebase still refuses
.B apply
/
.B pop
outright, a deliberate divergence from real git (which allows it) taken
for consistency with
.BR switch / merge .
.PP
A stash created with
.B -u
or
.B -a
(three parents) is restored by
.B apply
/
.B pop
too: the untracked half is written back to disk, unstaged, creating any
directories it needs. Where real git, on a collision with the untracked
half, applies the tracked half anyway and then fails the untracked half
file-by-file \(em leaving the entry on the stack with its own files now in
the way, a state with no clean exit \(em sg folds the untracked half into
the same pre-flight collision check as the tracked half and refuses the
whole apply if anything on either half collides, writing nothing. This is
strictly safer (an apply either fully succeeds or touches nothing) at the
cost of not matching real git's partial-apply behavior here.
.PP
.B \-\-index
(after a
.I clean
merge only) additionally replaces the index outright with the stash's own
index tree \(em the exact staged state at the time of
.BR push \(em
instead of the rule above's per-path result. On conflict this step is
skipped, matching real git, and the index is left as the merge left it; the
caller is not told separately, so check for conflict markers.
.PP
.B drop
removes an entry from the stack without applying it.
.B clear
empties the whole stack. Both are no-ops (exit 0) if invoked with nothing
to remove.
.PP
Stdout messages that are scraped by scripts are copied verbatim from real
git:
.RB \(dq "No local changes to save" \(dq ,
.RB \(dq "Saved working directory and index state ..." \(dq ,
.RB \(dq "stash@{" N "}: ..." \(dq ,
and
.RB \(dq "Dropped ... (...hex...)" \(dq .
Error messages use
.BR sg 's
own (Chinese) wording and always exit 1;
.B sg
has no exit 128, so situations where real git uses 128 (e.g.
.RB \(dq "log for 'stash' only has N entries" \(dq )
become an
.B sg
style message with exit 1 instead.
.SS reflog
.B sg reflog
.RB [ show ]
.RI [ ref ]
.RB [ -n
.IR count ]
.PP
Prints the update history of a single ref, most-recent entry first is
.I not
the order used \(em like real git, entries print oldest-shown-last but
numbered newest-first: each line is
.RS
.IP
.I abbrev ref @{ N }: message
.RE
.PP
where
.I abbrev
is a fixed 7 hex digits (real git's
.B core.abbrev
auto-lengthens for large repositories;
.B sg
does not) of the entry's
.B new
object id \(em the value the ref was moved
.I to
\(em and
.I ref
is echoed back exactly as typed, not normalized (e.g.
.B "sg reflog heads/topic"
prints
.RB \(dq heads/topic@{0}: \(dq ,
not
.RB \(dq refs/heads/topic@{0}: \(dq ).
.I ref
defaults to
.B HEAD
if omitted;
.B show
is accepted and ignored, matching real git's default subcommand.
.B -n
.I count
caps the number of entries printed (newest first); without it every entry
is printed.
.PP
If
.I ref
does not resolve to anything, exit 1 with an error. If it resolves but has
no reflog on disk (never been logged, or the log file has been removed),
exit 0 and print nothing \(em \(dqno history yet\(dq is not an error, the
same distinction
.B "sg stash list"
makes for an empty stack.
.PP
This is a read-only subset of real git's
.BR "git reflog" :
no
.BR expire ,
.BR delete ,
.BR --date ,
.BR --format ,
or
.BR --all .
See
.B REVISIONS
below for
.BR "<ref>@{N}" ,
which resolves a single entry directly rather than listing the whole log.
.SH REVISIONS
Anywhere a command above takes a
.I rev
(or
.IR ref ),
it accepts one of the following, resolved by
.B sg_rev_parse_commit
and shared by every command that resolves a revision:
.TP
.B HEAD
The current branch's tip commit.
.TP
.I branch\-name\fR,\fP tag\-name
Resolved against
.BR refs/heads/ ,
then
.BR refs/tags/ ,
then a bare branch lookup, in that order.
.TP
a full 40\-character hex object id
Abbreviated (partial) hex ids are
.B not
supported; this is a deliberate omission, not an oversight \(em the full id
must be given.
.PP
Any of the above may be followed by
.IR "~N" ,
.IR "^N" ,
or
.IR "@{N}" ,
and these may be chained (e.g.
.BR "topic~2^1" ).
.TP
.BI ~ N
The
.IR N th
first-parent ancestor (
.B ~
alone means
.BR ~1 ).
.TP
.BI ^ N
The
.IR N th
parent of a merge commit (
.B ^
alone means
.BR ^1 ;
.B ^0
is the commit itself).
.TP
.BI @{ N }
The
.IR N th
most recent entry in
.IR ref 's
own reflog, counting back from the current value (
.B @{0}
is the value the ref was most recently moved
.I to
\(em not the value before that move; see
.B reflog
above). Must immediately follow the ref name \(em
.B topic@{1}~1
is valid,
.B topic~1@{1}
is not, since the base-name scan stops at the first
.B ~
or
.BR ^ ,
leaving
.RB \(dq @{1} \(dq
to be rejected by the
.BR ~ / ^
suffix parser instead of read as a reflog selector.
.IR N
must be purely decimal digits;
.B @{}
(empty),
.BR @{u} ,
.BR @{upstream} ,
and any other non-numeric content inside the braces are rejected outright.
.B sg
does not support git's
.B @{<date>}
selector \(em a very large
.I N
is still read as an index, not a date, so
.B sg
and real git can disagree on numbers past roughly 100000000 (real git starts
treating them as Unix timestamps around then; the exact boundary is a
heuristic in real git, not a documented cutoff, so
.B sg
does not attempt to replicate it and simply always reads
.I N
as an index).
.TP
.B @
On its own,
.BR HEAD ,
including with suffixes \(em
.B @~1
is
.BR HEAD~1 .
This holds even if a branch is literally named
.BR @ .
.TP
.BI @{ N }
.RI "(with no ref name in front of it)"
The
.IR N th
most recent entry in
.B the current branch's
reflog \(em
.B not
.BR HEAD 's.
The two are genuinely different files and give different answers once a
checkout away and back has appended to
.B logs/HEAD
without touching the branch's own log. On a detached
.B HEAD
there is no branch and
.B logs/HEAD
is used instead; on an unborn
.B HEAD
the expression is rejected, as it is in real git.
.IP
One measured case is deliberately not reproduced: if the current branch's
reflog file is missing entirely, real git lets
.B @{0}
fall back to the branch's own tip while still rejecting the spelled-out
.BR <branch>@{0} .
.B sg
rejects both.
.SH TRANSPORTS
.B sg
reaches a remote two ways.
.PP
An
.BR http:// / https://
url uses the git smart HTTP protocol.
.PP
An
.B ssh://[user@]host[:port]/path
url, or the scp-like shorthand
.BR [user@]host:path ,
runs
.B git\-upload\-pack
or
.B git\-receive\-pack
on the far side through
.BR ssh .
The two spellings differ in one place worth knowing: after
.BR ssh:// ,
the path keeps its leading slash
.RB ( ssh://host/srv/x
asks for
.IR /srv/x )
while the shorthand has none to keep
.RB ( host:srv/x
asks for
.IR srv/x );
and a path beginning
.B ~
drops the slash so the far side's shell expands it. The shorthand has no
port: in
.BR host:22 ,
.B 22
is the path.
.PP
The ssh program is taken from
.B GIT_SSH_COMMAND
(split on whitespace, as git does), then
.BR GIT_SSH ,
then
.BR ssh .
.B core.sshCommand
is not read. Anything ssh prints \(em host key prompts, authentication
failures \(em reaches the terminal unchanged, because
.B sg
has no way to authenticate on its own.
.PP
Unlike git,
.B sg
opens a separate connection for the ref advertisement and for the transfer
that follows, rather than holding one open across both.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B .gitignore
Per-directory ignore rules, read by
.B status
and
.BR add .
A
.B .gitignore
in any directory applies to that directory and everything below it, and a
deeper file's rules take precedence over a shallower one's; within one
file, the last matching pattern decides. Supported syntax matches
.BR gitignore (5):
comments
.RB ( # ),
negation
.RB ( ! ),
directory-only patterns (trailing
.BR / ),
anchoring (a
.B /
anywhere except at the end anchors the pattern to that file's directory,
otherwise it matches by basename at any depth),
.BR * ,
.BR ? ,
character classes
.RB ( [a-c] ,
.BR [!a-c] ),
and
.B **
as a whole path segment. A pattern that excludes a directory buries
everything inside it \(em no negation can re-include content beneath an
excluded directory. Ignore rules never apply to files already tracked in
the index.
.TP
.B .git/info/exclude
Repository-local ignore rules with the same syntax, consulted at lower
precedence than any
.BR .gitignore .
The
.B core.excludesFile
setting and
.B ~/.gitconfig
are
.B not
read, so a global ignore file configured for
.B git
has no effect on
.BR sg .
.TP
.B .git/config
Read for
.B sg
specific settings under an
.B [sg]
section, and for
.B [remote \(dqNAME\(dq]
.B url
(used by
.BR fetch / push ).
Only the repository's own
.B .git/config
is consulted \(em
.B sg
does not read a user-global
.B ~/.gitconfig
or any system-wide git config file.
.RS
.TP
.B chunking
Boolean (\c
.B true
or any other value/absent, treated as false). When true, files added via
.B sg add
whose content is at or above the
.B chunkthreshold
are stored using content-defined chunking instead of as an ordinary blob.
.B Default: false (chunking is off).
.TP
.B chunkthreshold
A positive integer, in bytes. The minimum content size at or above which
.B sg add
chunks a file's blob when
.B chunking
is true. Ignored (and left at its default) if absent, zero, or
unparseable. Default: 4194304 (4 MiB).
.TP
.B everchunked
Boolean, set to
.B true
automatically the first time this repository (locally, or via a
.BR clone / fetch
that pulled in a remote's chunk data) actually produces or receives a
chunked blob. This is a durability marker, not a user-facing setting: once
set, its absence later (e.g. from an edited or reconstructed config) is
treated as evidence that
.B refs/sg/chunks
has been lost, and every chunk pointer in the repository is then treated as
broken rather than silently re-interpreted as ordinary content. It should
not be hand-edited.
.RE
.TP
.B refs/sg/chunks
An
.B sg
specific keep-alive ref (a commit, not a branch or tag) that references
every chunk blob any file in this repository has ever been split into.
Its sole purpose is to keep chunk blobs reachable from git's object graph
so that
.B git gc
(run by real
.B git
or by any other tool) does not collect them as garbage \(em without it, a
chunk pointer blob's referenced chunks could be silently deleted out from
under it.
.B Must not be deleted once chunking has been used
in a repository: if
.B refs/sg/chunks
is deleted or otherwise lost while
.B everchunked
is still recorded as true, every chunked file becomes unrecoverable and
.B sg
fails hard (rather than silently returning pointer text as file content)
for each one. It is merged, not overwritten, by
.BR clone / fetch ,
and its update is bundled together with the branch ref update by
.B push
when the server supports atomic ref updates.
.TP
.B .git/sg-chunks.lock
A lock file (created with
.B O_CREAT|O_EXCL
and never read) used to serialize concurrent updates to
.B refs/sg/chunks
across concurrent
.B sg
invocations in the same repository (e.g. two
.B "sg add"
calls on different large files, or an
.B add
racing a
.BR fetch ).
Only its existence is meaningful, not its content. A lock held
by a process that was killed or crashed can be left behind; if
.B sg
repeatedly fails to acquire it (after roughly 4 seconds of retries), remove
it by hand once you have confirmed no other
.B sg
process is actually running against the repository.
Concurrency protection is local-machine only \(em it is not safe against
concurrent
.B sg
processes on different machines sharing the repository over a network
filesystem.
.TP
.B refs/small-git/undo/*
Where
.B sg
stores its automatic safety snapshots (see the
.B undo
command above), one ref per snapshot. Not intended to be read or edited
directly; use
.B "sg undo"
to list and restore them.
.TP
.B .git/sg-rebase/
Holds the on-disk state of an in-progress
.B sg rebase
(the original branch/commit, the upstream target, and the remaining
todo list). Present only while a rebase is paused or running; removed
automatically when the rebase finishes, is skipped to completion, or is
aborted. Not intended to be edited by hand \(em use
.BR "sg rebase --continue|--skip|--abort" .
.TP
.B .git/CHERRY_PICK_HEAD \fR/\fP.git/REVERT_HEAD \fR, \fP.git/MERGE_MSG \fR, \fP.git/sequencer/
Holds the on-disk state of a paused
.B "sg cherry-pick"
(the first of the pair,
.BR CHERRY_PICK_HEAD )
or a paused
.B "sg revert"
(the second,
.BR REVERT_HEAD ;
exactly one of the two exists at a time):
it names the stopped commit, and
.B MERGE_MSG
holds the message it would have produced (with a
.B "# Conflicts:"
block appended when the pause was a conflict, not an empty pick). Picking
or reverting more than one commit at once additionally creates
.BR .git/sequencer/ ,
listing the remaining commits still to replay
.RB ( pick
or
.B revert
lines, matching which command is running). Unlike
.BR .git/sg-rebase/ ,
this layout deliberately matches real git's own file names and format
exactly (only the id field width of
.B sequencer/todo
differs, 40 hex characters instead of git's abbreviated 7, since
.B sg
has no abbreviated-object-name resolution to read a short one back with),
so a real
.B git
binary can inspect, finish, or abort a pick/revert
.B sg
left paused. The reverse direction is more limited: a real
.B git
binary's own todo entries use its abbreviated 7-hex ids, which
.B sg
cannot resolve back to an object, so
.B sg
can
.B --abort
or
.B --quit
a pick/revert
.B git
left paused (both read only the two full-40-hex
.B sequencer/
files git also writes in full) but cannot
.BR --continue / --skip
it. Present only while a cherry-pick or revert is paused; removed when it
finishes, is skipped to completion, or is aborted. Not intended to be
edited by hand \(em use
.BR "sg cherry-pick --continue|--skip|--abort|--quit" " or " "sg revert --continue|--skip|--abort|--quit" .
.TP
.B refs/stash \fR(and\fP .git/logs/refs/stash\fR)\fP
Where
.B "sg stash"
keeps the stash stack: the ref points at the newest stash commit, and the
reflog file (one line per entry, oldest first) is the actual index used by
.BR stash@{ N } .
Both are the same real-git on-disk format \(em not
.BR sg -specific
\(em so entries created by
.B git stash
are readable by
.B "sg stash"
and vice versa. Not intended to be edited by hand; use
.B "sg stash"
/
.B "git stash"
to inspect or modify them.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
.TQ
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
Identity recorded as both the author and (unless overridden below) the
committer of commits created by
.BR commit ,
.BR merge ,
and
.BR rebase ,
and of the internal keep-alive commits chunked storage writes to
.BR refs/sg/chunks .
Default when unset or empty:
.B small_git
and
.BR sg@localhost ,
respectively. There is no config-file equivalent (no
.BR user.name / user.email
support) \(em these environment variables are the only way to set commit
identity.
.TP
.B GIT_COMMITTER_NAME
.TQ
.B GIT_COMMITTER_EMAIL
Committer identity used specifically for the internal keep-alive commits
that chunked storage writes to
.BR refs/sg/chunks ;
defaults to the same value as
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
if unset. Ordinary commits made by
.BR commit / merge / rebase
always use
.B GIT_AUTHOR_NAME
/
.B GIT_AUTHOR_EMAIL
for the committer field too (there is no separate committer override for
those).
.TP
.B SG_USERNAME
.TQ
.B SG_PASSWORD
HTTP basic-auth credentials used by
.BR clone ,
.BR fetch ,
and
.B push
when talking to a remote over
.BR http(s) .
If unset, credentials are still looked up from
.B ~/.netrc
(via libcurl's optional netrc support) for a matching host; a 401/403
response suggests both of these. There is no
.B sg
specific mechanism for token/OAuth authentication beyond what a URL's own
userinfo or these two variables provide.
.PP
.B sg
does not read or honor any proxy- or TLS-verification-specific environment
variable itself (no
.B SG_PROXY,
no
.B SG_NO_VERIFY_SSL,
etc.); TLS certificate verification is always left at libcurl's secure
defaults and is not configurable via
.B sg .
The underlying HTTP transport (libcurl) does, independently of
.BR sg 's
own code, honor the standard
.B http_proxy
/
.B https_proxy
/
.B no_proxy
environment variables if libcurl was built with that support, exactly as
.B curl(1)
itself would.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
Failure: an invalid invocation (bad flags/argument count), a repository
state that prevents the operation (dirty working directory, unresolved
conflicts, not a repository, etc.), an aborted confirmation prompt, an I/O
error, or a merge/rebase left paused on a conflict.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR git (1)
.PP
.BR docs/DESIGN.md
in the small_git source tree documents the project's design goals,
phased roadmap, and per-phase known limitations in more detail than this
manual page.
.SH BUGS
.B sg
implements a deliberately narrow subset of git's functionality (see
.B DESCRIPTION
above and small_git's
.B README.md
Known Limitations section); missing flags and
subcommands found in real git are not oversights to be reported unless
they contradict what is documented here.
.SH CAVEATS
Running
.B "sg stash"
(or
.BR push )
while an
.B sg rebase
is paused resets the working tree and index back to
.BR HEAD ,
which can make a subsequent
.B "sg rebase --continue"
conclude that the currently paused commit's change is already present
upstream and skip it \(em the commit silently disappears from the
resulting history, with the work preserved only inside the stash. This is
not an
.B sg
defect: real git 2.55.0 exhibits the identical behavior for
.B "git stash"
under the same circumstances (measured). Pop the stash back out, or finish
the rebase first, if this matters for a given commit.
